{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Dead Sea Microbialites: Halophiles at the Brink",
    "date": "2026-10-02",
    "lesson": "The Dead Sea (actually a hypersaline terminal lake, not a sea) sits at roughly 34% salinity, nearly ten times normal seawater, pushing salt tolerance mechanisms to their physiological limit. Only extreme halophilic archaea and bacteria survive here, using compatible solutes and ion pumps to avoid osmotic collapse. Nutrients are scarce and episodic, arriving mainly after rare flash floods wash organic matter and freshwater into the lake, briefly freshening surface layers before evaporation restores the salt load. This scenario models that boom-bust rhythm: a thin, carbohydrate-and-protein-rich pulse of flood-borne organic matter is grazed down fast by a sparse, slow community adapted to withstand crushing ionic stress, in water tinted deep red-orange by carotenoid-pigmented halophiles.",
    "citation": "Oren, A. (2010) Industrial and environmental applications of halophilic microorganisms. Environ Technol.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Dead Sea hypersaline lake, Israel/Jordan - halophilic archaea/bacteria bloom after winter flood freshening events"
  },
